  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nA weak cold front moving through tonight will shift winds to the\nnorthwest on Tuesday. Despite the frontal passage, minimal low-\nlevel air mass contrast will keep temperatures mild, with highs \nsimilar to today, ranging from around 60 degrees north of I-80 \nto the low-to-mid 60s to the south. \n\nA broader pattern shift will unfold across North America during\nthe mid-to-late week period. Upper-level ridging over western \nNorth America will carve out a trough across the eastern U.S. \nThursday into Friday, bringing cooler and more unsettled weather\nto our region. However, ensemble guidance suggests the upper \nridge will expand eastward into the central and eastern U.S. by \nearly next week as a deep trough develops over the eastern \nPacific into western North America. Consequently, after a couple\nof days of colder weather with scattered showers, temperatures \nare expected to moderate back above normal early next week. \n\nThe amplifying upper-level pattern will drive a compact \nshortwave east- southwestward across the Mid-Mississippi Valley \non Wednesday. Model and ensemble guidance continue to target a \nsurface low track somewhere over central Illinois into Indiana \nWednesday afternoon and evening. This keeps the highest rain \nchances (60%+) primarily south of I-80. A few thunderstorms \ncannot be ruled out late Wednesday as lapse rates steepen ahead \nof the low, but these chances also remain focused south of I-80 \ninto central Illinois and Indiana. \n\nIn the wake of Wednesday's system, several additional shortwave\nimpulses diving into the Upper Midwest and western Great Lakes \nwill merge into a broader trough across the eastern half of the \ncountry Thursday into Friday. A colder airmass will shift over \nthe area for the end of the week accompanied by gusty northerly \nwinds. While shower chances will persist on Thursday for most \nareas, lake-induced thermodynamics will foster a transition \ntoward primarily lake- effect precipitation late Thursday into \nFriday. Uncertainty remains regarding the low-level flow \norientation (NNE vs. NNW/NW), which will determine how far west \nlake-effect showers drift into northeast Illinois versus \nremaining focused over northwest Indiana. Precipitation type \nalso remains uncertain Thursday night into Friday morning, when \ntemperatures may become cold enough to support a period of wet \nsnow, particularly just inland from the lake in northwest \nIndiana.\n\nA strong surface high expanding eastward over the weekend into \nearly next week will bring an end to lake-effect showers, \nleading to another stretch of dry weather with temperatures \ngradually moderating back into the 50s.\n\nKJB",
